WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.153
An image service accepts twenty uploads per second
during a burst.

00:00:04.153 --> 00:00:09.124
Each preview worker completes five jobs per second
under the assumed image mix.

00:00:09.124 --> 00:00:15.165
Two workers can process ten jobs per second, so the
backlog grows by approximately ten jobs each

00:00:15.165 --> 00:00:16.739
second during that burst.

00:00:16.739 --> 00:00:22.906
Adding two more workers could match the arrival rate
if shared storage and other dependencies have

00:00:22.906 --> 00:00:23.849
spare capacity.

00:00:23.849 --> 00:00:30.072
The queue exposes the difference between accepting
work and providing enough capacity to finish it.

00:00:30.072 --> 00:00:36.296
Keep admission through the service API, where
authorization, validation and workload limits
belong,

00:00:36.296 --> 00:00:40.633
rather than letting public clients publish arbitrary
broker messages.

00:00:40.633 --> 00:00:46.919
Producers and consumers can scale separately, but
the queue itself and downstream storage still have

00:00:46.919 --> 00:00:47.359
limits.

00:00:47.359 --> 00:00:53.061
A larger queue changes how long work can wait; it
does not increase processing throughput.

00:00:53.061 --> 00:00:59.207
Measure task duration by job type, since a thousand
small previews may cost less than a few large

00:00:59.207 --> 00:00:59.524
ones.

00:00:59.524 --> 00:01:05.227
Scale against useful completion rate and queue age,
and test whether adding workers shifts

00:01:05.227 --> 00:01:06.558
contention elsewhere.

00:01:06.559 --> 00:01:12.715
Suppose a report queue holds six hundred pending
jobs and its workers finish ten jobs per second

00:01:12.715 --> 00:01:15.023
while new arrivals temporarily stop.

00:01:15.023 --> 00:01:17.332
A rough drain time is sixty seconds.

00:01:17.332 --> 00:01:23.296
If arrivals continue at eight jobs per second, the
net drain rate is only two, stretching the

00:01:23.296 --> 00:01:24.899
estimate to five minutes.

00:01:24.899 --> 00:01:31.470
These simplified calculations explain why queue
length alone is insufficient: arrival rate, service

00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:35.387
rate and the age of waiting work determine user
experience.

00:01:35.387 --> 00:01:38.374
Set an admission policy before storage fills.

00:01:38.374 --> 00:01:44.879
Depending on the product, reject excess work,
postpone low-priority jobs or coalesce requests that

00:01:44.879 --> 00:01:47.003
genuinely supersede one another.

00:01:47.003 --> 00:01:49.201
Recovery also needs ordering rules.

00:01:49.201 --> 00:01:55.355
Two changes to the same report configuration should
not accidentally apply in reverse order merely

00:01:55.355 --> 00:01:58.620
because different workers finish at different times.

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:04.836
A broker's guarantees depend on its configuration
and partitioning, so define the scope of required

00:02:04.836 --> 00:02:05.213
order.

00:02:05.213 --> 00:02:11.493
Bounded retries and a visible failed-job state
prevent one malformed message from consuming
capacity

00:02:11.493 --> 00:02:14.067
indefinitely while legitimate work waits.
